 9-card straight from 6 to Ace (`6-7-8-9-10-J-Q-K-A`), which is an exceptionally strong combination to clear the majority of my hand. Crucially, the card counting reveals that the Landlord played three 10s in the previous turn, and I hold the fourth 10. Since all 10s are accounted for, it is impossible for the Landlord (or my teammate) to form any straight (as a 10 is required to bridge the gap between low and high cards). This makes my straight unbeatable by any normal hand; the Landlord must use a Bomb to stop it.\n\nReason:\n1.  **Hand Clearing**: Playing the 9-card straight reduces my hand from 14 cards to 5 cards instantly, disposing of isolated singles (6, 8, Q, K, A) that would otherwise be difficult to play.\n2.  **Unbeatable Normal Play**: Because all 10s are gone, the Landlord cannot match a straight. He is forced to pass or use a Bomb. Given he only has 8 cards left, forcing a Bomb is a tactical win (depleting his hand strength). If he passes, I am close to emptying my hand.\n3.  **Teammate Support**: My teammate has 17 cards and is likely holding strength to cover my remaining small cards (pair of 3s, singles 7, 9, J) if necessary, but this move puts maximal pressure on the Landlord immediately.\n\nAnswer: {"cards": ["♣6", "♦7", "♠8", "♦9", "♦10", "♣J", "♠Q", "♠K", "♠A"]}'}
